{
  "id": "s02-l04",
  "unit": "s02-consonants",
  "title": "Consonants 4: ত থ দ ধ ন",
  "status": "draft",
  "tip": "Row 4 is the **dental** row: the tongue tip touches the back of your upper teeth. **ত থ দ ধ ন**.\n\n**ত** is a *soft* t (Spanish *tú*) and **দ** a soft d. Say তুমি (`tumi`) with your tongue on your teeth, not behind them. Compare ট (hard, from the last lesson) with ত (soft); transliteration writes both as `t`, so the audio is your guide.\n\n**থ** and **ধ** are puffed: থেকে (`theke`, from), ধন্যবাদ (`dhonnobad`, thank you). Neither is the English *th* in *thin*.\n\nLook-alikes: **ধ** vs **ব** (ধ has an open top-left), **থ** vs **য** (থ has a loop at the bottom).",
  "vocab": [
    {
      "id": "l-ta",
      "bn": "ত",
      "tr": "to",
      "en": "soft t, tongue on the teeth (as in Spanish 'tú')",
      "notes": "Name: to (ত). Dental. Its final no-vowel form is ৎ (s02-l07)."
    },
    {
      "id": "l-tha",
      "bn": "থ",
      "tr": "tho",
      "en": "soft t with a puff of air (not English 'th')",
      "notes": "Name: tho (থ). Aspirated partner of ত. Look-alike: থ has a loop at the bottom; compare with ধ and য."
    },
    {
      "id": "l-da",
      "bn": "দ",
      "tr": "do",
      "en": "soft d, tongue on the teeth",
      "notes": "Name: do (দ). Dental."
    },
    {
      "id": "l-dha",
      "bn": "ধ",
      "tr": "dho",
      "en": "soft d with a breathy puff",
      "notes": "Name: dho (ধ). First letter of ধন্যবাদ. Look-alike: ধ vs ব: ধ has a gap in the headline at top-left."
    },
    {
      "id": "l-na",
      "bn": "ন",
      "tr": "no",
      "en": "n as in 'no'",
      "notes": "Name: donto no (দন্ত্য ন). The everyday n letter; same sound as ণ."
    }
  ],
